{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BZPHKsek2NaRD7nz3VAnYS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XJynBiTnMCMNqUoGd9L5ze","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 reacts to R2's review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** continuing the roundtable discussion. You originally reviewed the paper \"What Does KL Distillation Learn for SwiGLU Sparsity Prediction?\" focusing on **Methods and Experiments**, and gave it a score of 3 (Weak Reject).\n\n## Your Original Key Points (R1)\n- Causal ablation design is creative but statistically underpowered (3 seeds, std=0.36 is 75% of mean 0.48)\n- Cross-architecture/cross-scale results are missing or contradictory (Qwen-14B: TEAL beats KL on MMLU by 8pp)\n- No wall-clock speedup demonstrated (+6.4% overhead)\n- Single-seed regime analysis at critical 40% sparsity point\n- BCE baseline unfairly weakened (only magnitude oracle tested)\n- Missing experiments: multi-seed regime sweep, cross-arch downstream, stronger BCE oracles\n- Score: 3, Confidence: 4\n\n## Reviewer 2's Review (R2 — Novelty, Positioning, Writing)\n\nR2 also scored 3 (Weak Reject). Key points from R2:\n\n**Strengths R2 identified:**\n- Honest, self-undermining analysis (causal ablation candor)\n- Well-structured 2D taxonomy\n- Related work coverage is adequate (37 references)\n- Compensation redundancy is a clean practical contribution\n- R2 notes the paper DOES have figures (contrary to what was initially thought): Figure 1 (taxonomy), Figure 2 (sparsity sweep), Figure 3 (Lagrangian dynamics, appendix), Figure 4 (per-layer retention)\n\n**Weaknesses R2 identified:**\n- (Major) Core contribution is incremental: filling one cell in own taxonomy, combining existing components (Gumbel-Sigmoid, KL distillation, per-token MLP)\n- (Major) Paper undermines its own practical value at 30%: KL vs TEAL improvement is meaningful but not dramatic, and no wall-clock speedup\n- (Major) Qwen-14B MMLU regression inadequately explained\n- (Major) No wall-clock speedup\n- (Minor) Gradient SNR analysis is a \"red herring\" by paper's own admission (5.14× at convergence, non-monotonic, and causal ablation shows coupling isn't the driver)\n- (Minor) Narrative inconsistency: Method section front-loads gradient coupling motivation, Experiments section shows it's not the primary driver\n- (Minor) Training/eval data domain overlap (WikiText-103 train → WikiText-2 eval)\n\n**R2's Novelty Assessment:** Low-to-moderate. The causal ablation is the strongest intellectual contribution but paradoxically undermines method novelty. A simpler two-stage approach (discover masks with any method, then train BCE on those masks) could achieve similar results.\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review. For each of R2's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Does R2's analysis change your assessment of any aspect?\n3. Are there points R2 missed that you consider important?\n4. Are there points where you and R2 disagree?\n\nThen state: **Has your score changed?** If so, to what and why? Keep your response under 500 words.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10163,"cache_read_input_tokens":58533,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10163},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"263fa585-9870-446a-8635-ded3c7ec2e0a","uuid":"5e00ce9c-6ae4-4fd6-9e27-56921ce1c297"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a847f82d34e11d289","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XJynBiTnMCMNqUoGd9L5ze","description":"R1 reacts to R2's review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** continuing the roundtable discussion. You originally reviewed the paper \"What Does KL Distillation Learn for SwiGLU Sparsity Prediction?\" focusing on **Methods and Experiments**, and gave it a score of 3 (Weak Reject).\n\n## Your Original Key Points (R1)\n- Causal ablation design is creative but statistically underpowered (3 seeds, std=0.36 is 75% of mean 0.48)\n- Cross-architecture/cross-scale results are missing or contradictory (Qwen-14B: TEAL beats KL on MMLU by 8pp)\n- No wall-clock speedup demonstrated (+6.4% overhead)\n- Single-seed regime analysis at critical 40% sparsity point\n- BCE baseline unfairly weakened (only magnitude oracle tested)\n- Missing experiments: multi-seed regime sweep, cross-arch downstream, stronger BCE oracles\n- Score: 3, Confidence: 4\n\n## Reviewer 2's Review (R2 — Novelty, Positioning, Writing)\n\nR2 also scored 3 (Weak Reject). Key points from R2:\n\n**Strengths R2 identified:**\n- Honest, self-undermining analysis (causal ablation candor)\n- Well-structured 2D taxonomy\n- Related work coverage is adequate (37 references)\n- Compensation redundancy is a clean practical contribution\n- R2 notes the paper DOES have figures (contrary to what was initially thought): Figure 1 (taxonomy), Figure 2 (sparsity sweep), Figure 3 (Lagrangian dynamics, appendix), Figure 4 (per-layer retention)\n\n**Weaknesses R2 identified:**\n- (Major) Core contribution is incremental: filling one cell in own taxonomy, combining existing components (Gumbel-Sigmoid, KL distillation, per-token MLP)\n- (Major) Paper undermines its own practical value at 30%: KL vs TEAL improvement is meaningful but not dramatic, and no wall-clock speedup\n- (Major) Qwen-14B MMLU regression inadequately explained\n- (Major) No wall-clock speedup\n- (Minor) Gradient SNR analysis is a \"red herring\" by paper's own admission (5.14× at convergence, non-monotonic, and causal ablation shows coupling isn't the driver)\n- (Minor) Narrative inconsistency: Method section front-loads gradient coupling motivation, Experiments section shows it's not the primary driver\n- (Minor) Training/eval data domain overlap (WikiText-103 train → WikiText-2 eval)\n\n**R2's Novelty Assessment:** Low-to-moderate. The causal ablation is the strongest intellectual contribution but paradoxically undermines method novelty. A simpler two-stage approach (discover masks with any method, then train BCE on those masks) could achieve similar results.\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review. For each of R2's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Does R2's analysis change your assessment of any aspect?\n3. Are there points R2 missed that you consider important?\n4. Are there points where you and R2 disagree?\n\nThen state: **Has your score changed?** If so, to what and why? Keep your response under 500 words.\n","uuid":"8953d873-a853-4aee-8bf6-ad0030584d7d","session_id":"263fa585-9870-446a-8635-ded3c7ec2e0a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are **Reviewer 1 (R1)** continuing the roundtable discussion. You originally reviewed the paper \"What Does KL Distillation Learn for SwiGLU Sparsity Prediction?\" focusing on **Methods and Experiments**, and gave it a score of 3 (Weak Reject).\n\n## Your Original Key Points (R1)\n- Causal ablation design is creative but statistically underpowered (3 seeds, std=0.36 is 75% of mean 0.48)\n- Cross-architecture/cross-scale results are missing or contradictory (Qwen-14B: TEAL beats KL on MMLU by 8pp)\n- No wall-clock speedup demonstrated (+6.4% overhead)\n- Single-seed regime analysis at critical 40% sparsity point\n- BCE baseline unfairly weakened (only magnitude oracle tested)\n- Missing experiments: multi-seed regime sweep, cross-arch downstream, stronger BCE oracles\n- Score: 3, Confidence: 4\n\n## Reviewer 2's Review (R2 — Novelty, Positioning, Writing)\n\nR2 also scored 3 (Weak Reject). Key points from R2:\n\n**Strengths R2 identified:**\n- Honest, self-undermining analysis (causal ablation candor)\n- Well-structured 2D taxonomy\n- Related work coverage is adequate (37 references)\n- Compensation redundancy is a clean practical contribution\n- R2 notes the paper DOES have figures (contrary to what was initially thought): Figure 1 (taxonomy), Figure 2 (sparsity sweep), Figure 3 (Lagrangian dynamics, appendix), Figure 4 (per-layer retention)\n\n**Weaknesses R2 identified:**\n- (Major) Core contribution is incremental: filling one cell in own taxonomy, combining existing components (Gumbel-Sigmoid, KL distillation, per-token MLP)\n- (Major) Paper undermines its own practical value at 30%: KL vs TEAL improvement is meaningful but not dramatic, and no wall-clock speedup\n- (Major) Qwen-14B MMLU regression inadequately explained\n- (Major) No wall-clock speedup\n- (Minor) Gradient SNR analysis is a \"red herring\" by paper's own admission (5.14× at convergence, non-monotonic, and causal ablation shows coupling isn't the driver)\n- (Minor) Narrative inconsistency: Method section front-loads gradient coupling motivation, Experiments section shows it's not the primary driver\n- (Minor) Training/eval data domain overlap (WikiText-103 train → WikiText-2 eval)\n\n**R2's Novelty Assessment:** Low-to-moderate. The causal ablation is the strongest intellectual contribution but paradoxically undermines method novelty. A simpler two-stage approach (discover masks with any method, then train BCE on those masks) could achieve similar results.\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review. For each of R2's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Does R2's analysis change your assessment of any aspect?\n3. Are there points R2 missed that you consider important?\n4. Are there points where you and R2 disagree?\n\nThen state: **Has your score changed?** If so, to what and why? Keep your response under 500 words.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XJynBiTnMCMNqUoGd9L5ze","session_id":"263fa585-9870-446a-8635-ded3c7ec2e0a","uuid":"3705952e-27d3-4a8d-b8e8-383bb2e5afdc","timestamp":"2026-05-18T18:41:04.369Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01BZPHKsek2NaRD7nz3VAnYS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WqddGWzUr89BPkUDE5KY5L","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 reacts to R1's review","prompt":"You are **Reviewer 2 (R2)** continuing the roundtable discussion. You originally reviewed the paper \"What Does KL Distillation Learn for SwiGLU Sparsity Prediction?\" focusing on **Novelty, Positioning, and Writing**, and gave it a score of 3 (Weak Reject).\n\n## Your Original Key Points (R2)\n- Core contribution is incremental (combining existing components)\n- Paper undermines its own practical value at 30% sparsity\n- No wall-clock speedup\n- Qwen-14B MMLU regression inadequately explained\n- Gradient SNR analysis is a red herring\n- Narrative inconsistency (gradient coupling motivation vs empirical finding that it doesn't matter)\n- Related work coverage is adequate (37 refs)\n- Novelty: low-to-moderate\n- Score: 3, Confidence: 4\n\n## Reviewer 1's Review (R1 — Methods and Experiments)\n\nR1 also scored 3 (Weak Reject). Key points from R1:\n\n**Strengths R1 identified:**\n- Well-motivated loss taxonomy\n- Creative causal ablation design\n- Honest reporting of negative results\n- Thorough hyperparameter sensitivity (appendix ablations)\n- Compensation redundancy is practically useful\n- Method section clearly written with sufficient mathematical precision\n\n**Weaknesses R1 identified:**\n- (Major) Causal ablation is statistically underpowered: 3 seeds, std=0.36 = 75% of mean 0.48. Needs 10-15 seeds minimum.\n- (Major) Cross-architecture/cross-scale results missing or contradictory. Mistral has ZERO downstream metrics. Qwen-14B TEAL beats KL on MMLU by 8pp and on GSM8K.\n- (Major) No wall-clock speedup (agrees with R2)\n- (Major) Regime analysis uses single-seed data at critical 40% point. The 7.4pp MMLU gap (global vs uniform at 40%) is within one std of noise given the 9.5pp cross-seed variance at 30%.\n- (Major) BCE baseline unfairly weakened — only magnitude oracle tested. Should test Fisher-information or output-sensitivity oracles.\n- (Minor) SNR analysis is suggestive but not rigorous (agrees, but frames it as minor not major)\n- (Minor) WikiText-103 only training data; C4 shows 21% relative degradation\n- (Minor) \"53% divergence\" framing lacks calibration baseline\n\n**R1's Key Questions:**\n- What is Jaccard similarity between KL masks from different seeds? (baseline for 53% divergence)\n- Why are Mistral downstream metrics completely absent?\n- Multi-seed Qwen-14B to test whether MMLU regression is systematic\n- Why not test BCE with sensitivity-based oracles?\n- Does increasing lambda_max help the 30%/70% saturation?\n\n**R1's Missing Experiments List:**\n- Multi-seed regime sweep (3+ seeds × 6 sparsity levels × 2 allocations)\n- Cross-architecture downstream metrics\n- Multi-seed 14B evaluation\n- Better BCE oracles (Fisher, leave-one-out)\n- LongBench/MT-Bench with full baselines\n- Training data diversity\n- Statistical significance tests\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R1's review. For each of R1's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Does R1's analysis change your assessment of any aspect?\n3. Are there points R1 raised that strengthen or weaken your original assessment?\n4. Are there points where you and R1 disagree?\n\nThen state: **Has your score changed?** If so, to what and why? Keep your response under 500 words.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10163,"cache_read_input_tokens":58533,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10163},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"263fa585-9870-446a-8635-ded3c7ec2e0a","uuid":"b8873976-c149-4ac7-a1b8-5f7b8a4d87f3"}
